NAME
    modules - what a command module may ask of the shell, and the
    settings service it may be handed

DESCRIPTION
    A command module is a library of commands the shell links or loads.
    Its manifest names its commands, the job prefixes it claims, and the
    services it asks for; the shell hands each call only the services
    its host provides. Since version 1.1 of the module ABI a manifest
    may also name settings, and the shell guards one service for it.

THE SETTINGS SERVICE
    emusen.settings, version 1, is the program's runtime: a console's
    setting or a game's own, read and written with the runtime's own
    checks, and a core library approved or its approval revoked. A
    program that has a runtime provides it, as Mistress's shell does;
    the shell alone does not, and a command that needs it says so.

    A module never reaches it directly. Its manifest names the keys it
    may read and write ("settings", such as Engine for the cores
    command) and whether it may approve and revoke ("approvals"). The
    shell hands the module a table of its own over the program's that
    lets through only those keys; a module that names none is handed no
    settings service at all.

    An approval is a decision, so it is taken only from a command typed
    at the prompt: one run by a script given to source, inside $(...),
    or fed by a pipe is refused, as trust refuses one. Revoking an
    approval is allowed anywhere, since it only takes trust away.

EXAMPLES
    cores default "PlayStation 2" Sapphire
    cores default --game /home/Games/ps2/jak.chd -
    cores approve pcsx2

SEE ALSO
